Helios DXVK fork: keep empty mapped-pool chunks until the timer or the limits free them

Conduit guest/windows. Applied after 0019 by ci/windows/Build-Driver.ps1.

freeEmptyChunksInPool freed an empty chunk at once when it was smaller than
the pool's next chunk size. The mapped pool's chunks hold dynamic buffers'
discard slices, which fill and drain them every few frames, so a chunk could
be freed and allocated again moments later. On NVK over RM each of those is a
host round trip (an RM allocation and a mapping into the guest), and the
allocation happens inside Map(WRITE_DISCARD) on the game's render thread: CS2
spent about 7% of its render thread under DxvkMemoryAllocator::allocateMemory.

The mapped pool now frees empty chunks only by the existing rules that do not
depend on the chunk's size: more than four max-size chunks unused, unused for
20 seconds, or the heap budget. Device-local pools are unchanged.

diff --git a/src/dxvk/dxvk_memory.cpp b/src/dxvk/dxvk_memory.cpp
index e174bc46..bee66cbd 100644
--- a/src/dxvk/dxvk_memory.cpp
+++ b/src/dxvk/dxvk_memory.cpp
@@ -2031,7 +2031,17 @@ namespace dxvk {
       // Free the chunk if it is smaller than the current chunk size of
       // the pool, since it is unlikely to be useful for future allocations.
       // Also free if the pending allocation would exceed the heap budget.
-      bool shouldFree = chunk.memory.size < pool.nextChunkSize
+      //
+      // Helios: not for the mapped pool. Dynamic buffers (Map DISCARD) fill
+      // and drain its chunks every few frames, and on NVK over RM a chunk
+      // freed here and allocated again a moment later is a host round trip
+      // each way (RM allocation, map into the guest), on the game's render
+      // thread. Mapped chunks go by the unused-memory limit, the 20 s timer
+      // and the budget below instead.
+      const bool smallerThanNext = chunk.memory.size < pool.nextChunkSize
+        && &pool != &type.mappedPool;
+
+      bool shouldFree = smallerThanNext
         || allocationSize + heapAllocated > heapBudget
         || allocationSize > heapBudget;
 
